\section*{Cardy crossing probabilities for Poisson–Voronoi percolation}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents a proof in the selected manuscript. Annealed critical Poisson--Voronoi crossing probabilities are claimed to obey Cardy conformal formula in every bounded Jordan quadrilateral.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Result 1.} For full-plane Poisson--Voronoi cells at intensity $\varepsilon^{-2}$, independently colour each cell black with probability $1/2$. In a bounded Jordan domain $D$, let $a,b,c,d$ be distinct counterclockwise boundary marks; the crossing event requires a connected black subset of $\overline D$ meeting the closed arcs $ab$ and $cd$. Let $x\in(0,1)$ be the image of $b$ under the conformal map taking $(a,c,d)$ to $(0,1,\infty)$ in $\mathbb H$. Averaging both cell geometry and colours, the claim is
\[
 \lim_{\varepsilon\downarrow0}\mathbb P(\mathcal C_\varepsilon(D;a,b,c,d))
 =\frac{\int_0^x[u(1-u)]^{-2/3}\,du}{\int_0^1[u(1-u)]^{-2/3}\,du}
 =\frac{\Gamma(2/3)x^{1/3}}{\Gamma(1/3)\Gamma(4/3)}
 {}_2F_1\left(\frac13,\frac23;\frac43;x\right).
\]
This is the annealed scalar crossing statement, without extra boundary regularity beyond the Jordan condition.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Cluster-boundary circuits are decomposed into fully labeled dual components, including isolated edges, to obtain exact orthogonality. Local plugs open closed field sums, boundary potentials and a finite pin menu control transport along actual arcs, and these identities determine the crossing observable.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
